1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,840
A new AI model that can't write a single word.

2
00:00:03,940 --> 00:00:06,424
Yet everyone is talking about it.

3
00:00:06,774 --> 00:00:09,936
Let's follow one support ticket to find out.

4
00:00:10,036 --> 00:00:13,536
Three tiny decisions: which team, how urgent, how angry?

5
00:00:13,886 --> 00:00:16,031
Step one. Why the hype?

6
00:00:16,381 --> 00:00:20,221
Its launch video crossed 40 million views in a week.

7
00:00:20,321 --> 00:00:24,161
Vercel saw double its usual paid sign-ups in a day.

8
00:00:24,261 --> 00:00:27,423
$40M raised. Reportedly in talks at $10 billion.

9
00:00:27,523 --> 00:00:30,007
Built by ex-OpenAI researcher Diogo Almeida.

10
00:00:30,357 --> 00:00:32,841
Step two. How is it different?

11
00:00:33,191 --> 00:00:36,353
An LLM writes its answer, word by word.

12
00:00:36,453 --> 00:00:39,953
Jev never writes. It jumps straight to the decisions.

13
00:00:40,303 --> 00:00:43,126
Think exams. An LLM writes the essay.

14
00:00:43,226 --> 00:00:45,710
Jev just fills the OMR sheet.

15
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:48,633
It reads once, and answers everything together.

16
00:00:48,733 --> 00:00:50,878
Each with a confidence score.

17
00:00:51,228 --> 00:00:55,068
TypeSafe calls it a "System One" model: fast, gut-feel thinking.

18
00:00:55,418 --> 00:00:57,563
Step three. Speed and cost.

19
00:00:57,913 --> 00:01:01,075
In TypeSafe's own tests: up to 194x faster...

20
00:01:01,175 --> 00:01:03,997
...and 445x cheaper than a frontier model.

21
00:01:04,097 --> 00:01:08,615
Independent test by Every: 25x faster, 580x cheaper than Claude Fable 5.1.

22
00:01:08,965 --> 00:01:11,110
$0.042 per million input tokens.

23
00:01:11,210 --> 00:01:14,711
Output? Free. One dollar buys about 24 million tokens.

24
00:01:15,061 --> 00:01:17,545
Step four. Will it replace LLMs?

25
00:01:17,895 --> 00:01:21,057
No. It can't write emails, code or explanations.

26
00:01:21,407 --> 00:01:24,230
Jev decides. The LLM does the talking.

27
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:27,742
One catch: 100x cheaper, but called 1,000x more...

28
00:01:27,842 --> 00:01:29,986
...means a 10x bigger bill.

29
00:01:30,336 --> 00:01:32,820
Step five. Where is it useful?

30
00:01:33,170 --> 00:01:37,010
Anywhere apps make tiny decisions, thousands of times a day.

31
00:01:37,110 --> 00:01:40,272
Even checking if an AI agent is stuck.

32
00:01:40,622 --> 00:01:43,784
Mini project: let Jev route your support tickets.

33
00:01:43,884 --> 00:01:47,724
Confident? Route it. Not sure? Send it to a human.

34
00:01:47,824 --> 00:01:49,969
Our ticket: 97% billing. Routed.

35
00:01:50,319 --> 00:01:52,125
Step six. The rivals.

36
00:01:52,475 --> 00:01:56,653
Laya is free, open source, and runs on your own machine.

37
00:01:56,753 --> 00:01:59,915
Just 421M parameters. About 33 milliseconds per decision.

38
00:02:00,265 --> 00:02:05,122
Kev is another open one. Yes, the name is a joke on Jev.

39
00:02:05,472 --> 00:02:09,990
Why the silence? No big funding, no famous founder, no viral launch.

40
00:02:10,090 --> 00:02:13,591
And its own model card: about 36% out-of-the-box accuracy.

41
00:02:13,691 --> 00:02:16,514
It's a base you fine-tune, not plug-and-play.

42
00:02:16,864 --> 00:02:22,398
Worth it if your data must stay private and you have examples to train on.

43
00:02:22,498 --> 00:02:24,643
Want plug-and-play today? Not yet.

44
00:02:24,993 --> 00:02:26,799
Step seven. The verdict.

45
00:02:27,149 --> 00:02:30,311
The idea? Genuinely useful. Real teams use it.

46
00:02:30,411 --> 00:02:32,895
But most numbers are TypeSafe's own.

47
00:02:32,995 --> 00:02:35,479
Its own benchmark: about 68% accuracy.

48
00:02:35,579 --> 00:02:40,097
One test found Gemini slightly more accurate, but 10 to 20x pricier.

49
00:02:40,447 --> 00:02:44,286
Promising, not proven. Test it on your own data first.

50
00:02:44,636 --> 00:02:47,459
Save this for your next AI build.

51
00:02:47,809 --> 00:02:51,310
Yes, no, or depends? Tell me in the comments.

52
00:02:51,410 --> 00:02:53,555
Follow for more AI breakdowns.
